    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_00ce3217e78637f70aced560a162dcf79b851e1a1d85fa4b2d6feab5fb7318ed",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Convolution first rewrites the target analytic observable in coefficient form while keeping the missing singular data explicit: for zeta this is a Fourier-filtered Dirichlet series plus a pole residue, and for log zeta it is a von-Mangoldt series plus a correction from zeros to the right of the line. Under RH the latter correction vanishes at the ranges used for S and S1. When the kernel weights make the resulting Dirichlet coefficients nonnegative and absolutely summable, the resonance mean expands into nonnegative terms, so one may retain only a finite subcollection aligned with the resonator's approximate multiplicative relations; restricting the von-Mangoldt series to the prepared prime band then recovers the divisor statistic built into the resonator.",
