    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_3a258393e4d118fccfdd1e721ff9beca093c8dc2b3113d9ba9b852e22dc81375",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The authors attribute the improved detectable amplitude to a resonator using larger primes and a longer Dirichlet polynomial than earlier resonance work, but explicitly record two costs: the interval on which existence is obtained is considerably larger than in predecessor results, and no comparable estimate is known for the measure of the large-value set. In the zeta proof, a compact-frequency nonnegative kernel exposes a positive coefficient block, while second-moment localization, Gaussian tail decay, and the budget kappa<1-beta control the enlarged time range; the proof then gives existence of a large value and adjusts parameters back to the theorem's stated interval, not a prevalence statement.",
