    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_d7d020d6e46f86ab7a5c7f6fe4037f49e8bdbc660d212d36c9ef9308c9db79f7",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Kernel parity determines both which component of the conditional log-zeta formula is exposed and which sign conclusion resonance can preserve. For S, a real odd differentiated Gaussian has a purely imaginary Fourier transform; its prime-band imaginary weights can be made positive for Lemma 7, but the physical-side kernel changes sign, so the argument controls only a large absolute value of S. For S1, a real even Gaussian is nonnegative and has a positive real Fourier transform; after the extra logarithmic attenuation in the S1 coefficients, the same resonance mechanism preserves order and yields a one-sided positive extreme.",
